{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_kafh7pe2n4mphewy2ws6jjwpb7qar3slv2hlde2njlvn5kqvhrcq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The output matches the C version exactly. The build succeeds and the test passes.\n\nSummary of what I implemented in `src/coroutine.rs`:\n\nThe C code uses `ucontext_t` for stack-switching coroutines, which has no direct safe equivalent in Rust. I implemented the same cooperative-scheduling semantics using OS threads gated by per-coroutine `Mutex<CoState>` + `Condvar` pairs:\n\n- **`coroutine_open`**: Allocates a `Schedule` with `nco=0`, `cap=DEFAULT_COROUTINE`, `running=-1`.\n- **`coroutine_close`**: Joins any lingering coroutine threads, then drops the schedule.\n- **`coroutine_new`**: Mirrors the C slot-allocation logic — grow `cap` 2× when full, otherwise scan from `nco` for a free slot.\n- **`coroutine_resume`**: For `READY`, spawns a coroutine thread that waits on the condvar, then signals it. For `SUSPEND`, just signals the existing thread. Then blocks on the condvar until the coroutine yields or dies, mirroring `swapcontext`'s \"transfer control until I get it back\" semantics.\n- **`coroutine_yield`**: Sets the coroutine status to `SUSPEND`, signals main, and waits for resume.\n- **`coroutine_status` / `coroutine_running`**: Direct lookups.\n\nA small amount of `unsafe` is unavoidable because the public function signatures pass `&mut Schedule` into the user-supplied coroutine body, and the schedule itself owns all coroutine data — but the synchronization protocol guarantees that only one thread (main or one coroutine) ever touches the schedule at a time, so the access is sound."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":482,"cache_read_input_tokens":28124,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":482,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_m